00:21:19,284 --> 00:21:24,324
dropped on a deserted island and forced to kill each other until only one was left.

99
00:21:29,140 --> 00:21:58,140
It got people asking what they would do if they were in that situation. But the movie was not released in the west for years, leaving it an obscure cult classic. Then years later, Hunger Games came around, and it reignited the fire. People wanted battle royale games, but no one was making them. But one person wanted it more than anybody else.

100
00:21:58,140 --> 00:22:01,140
Brendan, PlayerUnknown, Green

101
00:22:01,140 --> 00:22:28,140
Brendan Green was not a game designer. He was an Irish photographer and web designer who followed the love of his life to Brazil. Two years later, he was divorced and looking to get his life back on track. He made it back home to Ireland. And while living on welfare, Green discovered the games Arma and DayZ. For those who don't know, Arma is a hyper-realistic military shooter, known for the scale of its combat, its attention to detail, and graphic design.

102
00:22:28,140 --> 00:22:44,140
Fidelity, also known for the game. Also known for the game. Also known for the game. Also known for being the foundation for an incredible number of mods. Many of which were spun off of into their own games. DayZ is one of the best known Arma mods, which use the hyper-realistic combat mechanics to simulate a zombie apocalypse.

103
00:22:44,140 --> 00:23:00,140
So yeah, I started off making a mod basically back in ARMA 2 because I wanted to play. I saw games like the survivor games, which were events for streamers, and I wanted to play that myself. So I decided to see if I could actually make one and ARMA 2 Battle Royale was born.

104
00:23:00,140 --> 00:23:19,140
The Battle Royale mod for ARMA 2 was a departure from the tightly directed experiences offered by AAA games like Assassin's Creed. Green was not interested in those types of games. He wanted something looser, something grittier. Something that gave you the freedom to show what you would do in a battle to the death.

105
00:23:19,140 --> 00:23:28,140
As DayZ broke free of ARMA 2 and ARMA 2 was a standalone game. Other developers took notice of how popular Green's mods were in the niche ARMA community.

106
00:23:28,140 --> 00:23:37,140
Sony Online Entertainment, now known as Daybreak, hired Green as a consultant on their own DayZ-style zombie survival game.

107
00:23:37,140 --> 00:23:48,140
That mode eventually became H1Z1 King of the Kill, but by February 2016, Green had moved on. He was headed to Korea, where he got an interesting offer.

108
00:23:48,140 --> 00:24:02,140
Chang Han Kim, a Korean game designer working for Bluehole, wanted to make a Battle Royale game. He was inspired by DayZ, but when he researched the Battle Royale format more and more, he kept coming across Green.

109
00:24:02,140 --> 00:24:17,140
And less than one month later, he was Bluehole's creative director. Development moved fast. Green and Kim's team had the autonomy to create the game that they wanted. And they wanted it to be playable within one year.

110
00:24:20,435 --> 00:24:27,435
The game started its development in early 2016, and the plan was to follow an early access schedule.

111
00:24:27,435 --> 00:24:34,435
Green and Kim wanted to get the game in customers hands fast for a low cost, and keep improving it over time.

112
00:24:34,435 --> 00:24:38,435
But the foundation needed to be laid down before anything else.

113
00:24:48,915 --> 00:25:03,076
The team made a small game, one map, one mode, a handful of weapons were added after the launch, but from the day it was released on Early Access in March 2017 to today, Battlegrounds has been the same game.

114
00:25:03,076 --> 00:25:13,875
However, when it first came out, there wasn't quite anything like it. Sure, Green's mods were still out there, but mods are never quite as popular as standalone games.

